{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>Please — this people has sinned a great sin, and they made themselves gods of gold.</b> There are several difficulties in this request. For what did Moses see to magnify the sin when he sought forgiveness — for this is not the way of any upright advocate? And furthermore, his words appear self-contradictory: for since he said, <b>and they made themselves gods of gold</b> — 'you caused it for them' and so on — if so, his intent was to diminish the sin. And furthermore, what is the meaning of <b>וַיַּעֲשׂוּ</b> with a vav? It implies as though he interrupted the subject, and its plain sense suggests that it is an explication of <b>a great sin</b>. And furthermore, why did he specify this sin more than the sin of forbidden relations and bloodshed that they committed, as Rashi explained on the verse וַיָּקוּמוּ לְצַחֵק?" "Now, we have already said that there was no actual forbidden relations and bloodshed there. And the proof for this is that what is explained — that they killed Hur — is impossible, for they killed him before the making of the calf, and the verse <b>וַיָּקוּמוּ לְצַחֵק</b> was stated after its making. Rather, since all the Decalogue statements were arranged five opposite five: <b>I am</b> opposite <b>You shall not murder</b>, <b>There shall not be for you</b> opposite <b>You shall not commit adultery</b> — and when they transgressed <b>I am</b> and <b>There shall not be for you</b>, it is as if they also transgressed <b>You shall not murder</b> and <b>You shall not commit adultery</b>, for whoever transgresses this falls in that. And we already find that bloodshed and forbidden relations are both called a great sin: for regarding bloodshed it is stated (Genesis 4:13) <b>my punishment is greater than I can bear</b>, and regarding forbidden relations it is stated (Genesis 39:9) <b>and how could I do this great evil</b>. If so, when Moses said <b>Please — this people has sinned a great sin</b>, he alluded to these two transgressions, forbidden relations and bloodshed, and afterwards he also confessed the sin of the calf and said <b>and they made themselves gods of gold</b>. And he did not wish to include it under the heading of forbidden relations and bloodshed, to indicate that specifically those two are great, but the sin of the calf is not so very great — for they do have an excuse, namely that you caused it for them on account of the abundance of gold that you bestowed upon them, which is not so with those two, for they have no relation to this excuse. And the entire intent of Moses was to diminish the sin of the calf, on account of which HaShem said to consume them; but on account of forbidden relations and bloodshed, even if you say that some of them transgressed these, nevertheless they are not all worthy of destruction on their account — and all the more so according to what we have written, that there was no actual forbidden relations and bloodshed there, as is evident from the language <b>לְצַחֵק</b>, and it does not say <b>וַיָּקוּמוּ וַיְצַחֲקוּ</b>, but rather that they committed the sin that leads to levity." "And he said, <b>'And now, if you will bear their sin, and if not, please erase me from your book.'</b> Since he said, 'Perhaps I shall atone for your sin,' he should have said, 'Perhaps HaShem will atone for your sin' — for it is not in Moses's hand to atone. And he should have said 'עַל חַטַּאתְכֶם' — what is the meaning of 'בְּעַד'? Rather, since Moses surrendered his life for Israel and said 'I shall atone,' he meant: here I am, an atonement on your behalf, for their leader is the guilt-offering of the people. Therefore he said <b>if you will bear their sin, and if not</b> — whether you bear it or do not bear it, <b>please erase me</b>. That is: if you bear their sin and take me as an atonement on their behalf — if so, erase me. And if you will not bear it, then also erase me, for how can I bear to see the destruction of my kindred?" "And according to its plain sense, it appears to me that the principal intent of the word 'וְעַתָּה' is as we find regarding the spies, where it is likewise written (Numbers 14:17) 'And now, let the strength of HaShem be great.' For the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses to consume them and make of him a great nation, and Moses said, 'Lest they say — from inability of HaShem' and so forth. And even when the seed of Moses becomes a great nation and they come to the Land, the nations will not be able to say 'from inability'; nevertheless, between Him and me the Name is profaned, and I desire 'And now let the strength of HaShem be great' also in those generations. So too Moses requested here, 'And now, if you will bear their sin' — and the verse is inverted, as is its manner in many places, as though he said: If you bear their sin — then 'and now': do this now and immediately, and do not dismiss me with 'go and return,' and let their seed come to the Land or my seed — for in any case, between Him and me the Name is profaned." "And it further appears to me that he did not seek to have the sin wiped away entirely, but only that the punishment should not be exacted all at once now, to visit destruction upon them this instant; rather, he would not be troubled if the punishment were exacted little by little, generation by generation. And HaShem granted him and said: 'And now, go, lead the people,' and so forth, 'and on the day of my visitation I will visit their sin upon them' — to exact payment from them little by little, for this is the lovingkindness of Elohim."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:12.440760960Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}